  3. THE PRE-EXISTING SKIP, AND WHY THE RENDERING IS ONE CASE AND NOT TWELVE
  The single skipped entity in this report records a CLASS LEVEL ignore annotation at line 99 of the test
  source, present at the base commit and untouched by this migration.
  The placement decides the shape of the whole file, so it was verified in context rather than taken on
  trust. A CLASS LEVEL ignore annotation makes the JUnit 4 runner route the entire class through its
  ignored class runner, which fires exactly ONE skip event and carries the CLASS description. The
  resulting report has tests 1, skipped 1, failures 0 and errors 0, and holds exactly one test case
  element whose name attribute and class name attribute are BOTH the fully qualified class name, with a
  single skipped child. A METHOD LEVEL ignore annotation would instead have produced a total equal to the
  declared method count, a skipped count equal to the ignored method count, and one skipped child per
  ignored method. This class takes the first form.
  Its twelve test methods are therefore NOT enumerated as individual test case elements, because the
  runner never reaches them. Emitting them would assert twelve outcomes that no run produced, and would
  misreport a class the runner treats as one indivisible skip.
  The skip is PRE-EXISTING, and it is CAPTURED, NOT RESOLVED. Pre-existing defects are documented rather
  than fixed, so nothing here removes the annotation, relocates it, records the class as passing, or
  quietly enumerates the twelve methods to make the report look fuller. No ignore annotation was added to
  or removed from any test class anywhere in the tree.
  A DORMANT TEST IS NOT A BASELINE FAILURE, and it may NEVER be cited as justification for a test
  exclusion. Only a failure observed at the baseline can justify one, and no failure is observed here.
  SKIPPED, FAILED and UNCOVERED are three distinct states, and this archive keeps them distinct rather
  than collapsing them into two: this class is SKIPPED; nothing in this module is recorded as FAILED; and
  the module's five integration classes, discussed in section 11, are UNCOVERED.

