  setting returns zero, and no retry plugin is declared anywhere, so a failure observed at the baseline is
  real and reproducible, and failing fast is safe. The reactor does carry a Spring retry APPLICATION
  library, declared as a root version property and consumed by exactly three child modules, which has
  nothing whatever to do with rerunning tests. In a task and workflow module, where retry semantics are
  part of the domain vocabulary, that inference is especially tempting and especially wrong.
  Second, coverage exclusions are NOT test exclusions. The root coverage plugin configuration excludes
  eight package globs from coverage MEASUREMENT only and disables no test, and none of the eight names a
  package of this module. Bindingly related: the coverage agent hands its configuration to the test runner
  through the default argument line property export, so the pinned unit test runner declaration must NOT
  set a literal argument line element. Setting one would silently sever instrumentation and leave the
  coverage check goal evaluating an empty data set, which reports a pass while measuring nothing.
  Instrumentation must be verifiably live. As it stands that declaration carries a version and nothing
  else, with neither a literal argument line nor an excludes entry, which is the correct state.
  A third reading is worth foreclosing while the first two are being foreclosed, because this is the one
  report here that invites it: the skipped element below is NOT a migration exclusion and NOT a
  suppression introduced by this work. It is the faithful rendering of an annotation that predates this
